{"name":"Cranbrook","hex":"#a65570","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Cranbrook","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A place in England:\nA new town and civil parish with a town council in East Devon district, Devon . \n\nA small town in Cranbrook and Sissinghurst parish, Tunbridge Wells borough, Kent .\nA suburb of Ilford, borough of Redbridge, Greater London .","A new town and civil parish with a town council in East Devon district, Devon ."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Cranbrook"}],"entries":[{"title":"Earl of Cranbrook","blocks":[{"text":"Earl of Cranbrook, in the County of Kent, is a title in the Peerage of the United Kingdom. It was created in 1892 for the Conservative politician Gathorne Gathorne-Hardy, Viscount Cranbrook. The family seat was Hemsted Park, near Benenden, Kent."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/Coronet_of_a_British_Earl.svg/330px-Coronet_of_a_British_Earl.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Earl_of_Cranbrook","wikidata":"Q5326241"},{"title":"Gathorne Gathorne-Hardy, 1st Earl of Cranbrook","blocks":[{"text":"Gathorne Gathorne-Hardy, 1st Earl of Cranbrook, was a prominent British Conservative politician. He held cabinet office in every Conservative government between 1858 and 1892."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/1st_Earl_of_Cranbrook.jpg/330px-1st_Earl_of_Cranbrook.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gathorne_Gathorne-Hardy%2C_1st_Earl_of_Cranbrook","wikidata":"Q5527395"},{"title":"Cranbrook (ward)","blocks":[{"text":"Cranbrook is an electoral ward in the London Borough of Redbridge. The ward has existed since the creation of the borough on 1 April 1965 and was first used in the 1964 elections. It returns councillors to Redbridge London Borough Council."}],"link":"h